# Image Capture Policy Decision Record

In the earlier investigation, the issue name and call name initially pointed to `src/pages/background.js`, so the initial approach was to look for the image capture policy near this file. The file header shows:

```js
import Log from '../js/lib/log.js';
import T from '../js/lib/tool.js';
import ExtApi from '../js/lib/ext-api.js';
import ExtMsg from '../js/lib/ext-msg.js';
```

At the time, there were two approaches: treat background.js directly as the rules entry point, or keep this path for now and only record the object relationships visible from it. The early decision leaned toward the former, without setting up a separate inspection entry point yet. This assessment did not spell out the boundaries between image, asset, and source, or specify which layer was responsible for the actual browser-side selection.

Open questions: where to confirm the current value of resource.; whether the priority relationship between attribute and source still follows the earlier account; leave any parts that haven't been rechecked blank.

# Ordered Snapshot Child Nodes: Experiment Notes

After review, the old "matching counts is enough" rule can no longer serve as a pass condition. Saving both children does not mean the order the caller receives is still usable; hidden nodes should not automatically be excluded from the records just because they are not visible on the page.

The file header currently shows a few adjacent dependencies:

```js
import T from '../lib/tool.js';
import ExtMsg from '../lib/ext-msg.js';
import {NODE_TYPE} from '../lib/constants.js';
import StyleSheetSnapshot from './stylesheet.js';
```

The style snapshot was not included in child node validation this time. It is only an adjacent object to keep in mind when reading the file; this round's samples still compare only node sequences and node categories.

MWC-215 are still missing the actual call associated with the most recent report and the result the user saw. So the next review will change only one variable: add a hidden node under the same parent, and save the input order and output order separately. Additional notes on the integration testing entry point go in DOC-26; we won't speculate about caller behavior here.
